     Levels with magic portals or extra stairs do not allow
bones.  I think it was more laziness than technical difficulty,
to just not bother about whether to fix up extra connections
that go to different places in the new dungeon than they did
in the old dungeon.  Think about finding the stairs to the
mines on level three, then finding a bones level on level four
where the previous player had his stairs to the mines on that
level.  (As things stand, the other player couldn't have left
bones for that level so this issue won't come up.)

> In which source/data file, exactly, and a line
> number reference would be helpful, do you find that
> "letter to level description" information, so I can
> go read it for myself? I tried but failed to find it
> earlier when discussing the naming of bones files.

     As has already been mentioned, dat/dungeon.def is what
you're looking for.  It has the bones designator letters
used when constructing file names.  src/dungeon.c has most
of the code dealing with that file (which gets processed by
util/makedefs and util/dgncomp as the program is being built;
the first strips out any conditional features that are being
excluded, the second parses the contents and produces a
binary file used by nethack itself).  The code to decide
whether to make a bones file is in src/bones.c.

     Dylan's gazetteer mentions which levels allow bones and
which don't
  http://www.spod-central.org/~psmith/nh/gazetteer/
although I don't think it includes the file naming details.


